er Scorpius. Its supernovae are thought to have blown the Local Bubble. The line joins the centres of the three subgroups (BANYAN Sigma models, Gagné et al. 2018); the label sits on Upper Centaurus-Lupus.","shape":{"kind":"polyline","points":[[-54.6,-6.7,-94],[-59,-72.1,-85.4],[-57.5,-108,-49]],"source":"gagne"}},{"id":"per-tau-shell","name":"Perseus-Taurus shell","aliases":["Per-Tau shell"],"type":"shell","noun":"Supernova-blown shell of gas and dust","rank":2,"x":115.4,"y":153.7,"z":102.2,"r":78,"positionSource":"bialy","facts":[{"label":"Radius","value":"78","unit":"pc","source":"bialy"},{"label":"Distance of the centre","value":"218","unit":"pc","source":"bialy"},{"label":"Age","value":"6 to 22","unit":"Myr","source":"bialy","approximate":true}],"description":"A near-spherical shell found in 3D dust maps, with the Perseus molecular cloud on its far side and Taurus on its near side; it was probably blown by supernovae. Drawn as the sphere of Bialy et al. 2021.","shape":{"kind":"sphere","source":"bialy"}},{"id":"gould-belt","name":"Gould Belt","aliases":["Gould's Belt"],"type":"region","noun":"Ring of bright young stars and clouds tilted to the Milky Way","rank":2,"x":5.3,"y":91.2,"z":49.8,"r":373,"positionSource":"perrot","facts":[{"label":"Semi-axes of the ring","value":"373 ± 5 and 233 ± 5","unit":"pc","source":"perrot"},{"label":"Tilt to the Galactic plane","value":"17.2 ± 0.5","unit":"°","source":"perrot"},{"label":"Distance of the centre","value":"104 ± 4","unit":"pc","source":"perrot"},{"label":"Expansion age (model)","value":"26.4 ± 0.4","unit":"Myr","source":"perrot"}],"description":"A ring of bright young stars and gas clouds tilted by about 20 degrees to the Milky Way, described since the 19th century and modelled here as an expanding ring (Perrot & Grenier 2003; the label sits at its centre, taken to lie in the Galactic plane). The Radcliffe Wave work (Alves et al. 2020) reinterprets it as a projection on the sky of two straight cloud complexes, the Radcliffe Wave and the Split, rather than a real ring."},{"id":"local-interstellar-cloud","name":"Local Interstellar Cloud","aliases":["LIC"],"type":"cloud","noun":"Warm cloud the Sun is passing through","rank":2,"x":0.75,"y":2.09,"z":1.35,"positionSource":"redfield","facts":[{"label":"Temperature","value":"7500 ± 1300","unit":"K","source":"redfield"},{"label":"Velocity relative to the Sun","value":"23.84 ± 0.90","unit":"km/s","source":"redfield"},{"label":"Flow direction (Galactic l, b)","value":"187° ± 3.4°, −13.5° ± 3.3°","source":"redfield"},{"label":"Area on the sky","value":"18270","unit":"square degrees","source":"redfield"},{"label":"Upper limit to the distance (nearest star with LIC absorption)","value":"2.6","unit":"pc","source":"redfield"}],"description":"A warm, partly ionised wisp of gas a few parsecs across; the Sun is inside it or at its edge, and its gas flows through the Solar System. The label sits towards the centre of the cloud on the sky (l = 170, b = -10) at 2.6 pc, the distance of the nearest star whose light shows the cloud, which is an upper limit to its distance (Redfield & Linsky 2008)."}]}
